# NestJS SOLID Principles

All code must follow the SOLID principles.

---

# S - Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

A class should have only one reason to change.

## Rules

- One class = one responsibility.
- Controllers handle HTTP requests only.
- Use Cases handle business logic only.
- Repositories handle data access only.
- Services should not mix unrelated responsibilities.

## Correct

```ts
export class CreateUserUseCase {
  async execute(dto: CreateUserDto) {}
}
```

```ts
export class UserRepository {
  async create(user: User) {}
}
```

## Wrong

```ts
export class UserService {
  async createUser() {}

  async sendEmail() {}

  async generateToken() {}

  async uploadAvatar() {}
}
```

This class has multiple responsibilities.

---

# O - Open Closed Principle (OCP)

Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.

## Rules

- Prefer interfaces and abstractions.
- Add new behavior through new implementations.
- Avoid modifying existing code when adding features.

## Correct

```ts
export interface PaymentProvider {
  process(amount: number): Promise<void>;
}
```

```ts
export class StripeProvider implements PaymentProvider {}
```

```ts
export class PaypalProvider implements PaymentProvider {}
```

## Wrong

```ts
if (provider === 'stripe') {
  ...
}

if (provider === 'paypal') {
  ...
}
```

Adding a provider should not require changing existing logic.

---

# L - Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

Subtypes must be replaceable for their base types.

## Rules

- Implementations must respect interface contracts.
- Never throw "Not Implemented" for required methods.
- Consumers should not care which implementation is used.

## Correct

```ts
export interface UserRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
}
```

```ts
export class TypeOrmUserRepository
  implements UserRepository {}
```

```ts
export class PrismaUserRepository
  implements UserRepository {}
```

Both can be swapped safely.

## Wrong

```ts
class PrismaUserRepository
  implements UserRepository {

  findById() {
    throw new Error('Not Implemented');
  }
}
```

---

# I - Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

Clients should not depend on methods they do not use.

## Rules

- Keep interfaces small and focused.
- Split large interfaces into specialized contracts.

## Wrong

```ts
export interface UserRepository {
  create();
  update();
  delete();
  sendEmail();
  uploadAvatar();
}
```

## Correct

```ts
export interface UserRepository {
  create();
  update();
}
```

```ts
export interface MailProvider {
  send();
}
```

```ts
export interface StorageProvider {
  upload();
}
```

---

# D - Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

High-level modules must not depend on low-level modules.

Both should depend on abstractions.

## Rules

- Depend on interfaces.
- Never inject database implementations directly into Use Cases.
- Use dependency injection everywhere.

## Correct

```ts
export class CreateUserUseCase {
  constructor(
    private readonly userRepository: IUserRepository,
  ) {}
}
```

## Wrong

```ts
export class CreateUserUseCase {
  constructor(
    private readonly repository: Repository<UserEntity>,
  ) {}
}
```

Use Cases must not depend on TypeORM.

---

# Controller Rules

Controllers must:

- Receive requests.
- Validate input.
- Call Use Cases.
- Return responses.

Controllers must not:

- Query databases.
- Contain business logic.
- Call TypeORM repositories directly.

## Correct

```ts
@Post()
create(@Body() dto: CreateUserDto) {
  return this.createUserUseCase.execute(dto);
}
```

## Wrong

```ts
@Post()
async create() {
  const user = await this.userRepository.save(...);

  await this.mailService.send(...);

  return user;
}
```

---

# Use Case Rules

Use Cases must:

- Contain business logic.
- Orchestrate workflows.
- Depend only on abstractions.

Use Cases must not:

- Access HTTP objects.
- Access Express Request or Response.
- Use TypeORM directly.

---

# Repository Rules

Repositories must:

- Access persistence layers.
- Map entities.
- Implement repository interfaces.

Repositories must not:

- Contain business logic.
- Send emails.
- Generate JWTs.

---

# Dependency Injection Rules

Always use constructor injection.

## Correct

```ts
constructor(
  private readonly userRepository: IUserRepository,
) {}
```

Avoid:

```ts
const repository = new UserRepository();
```

Never manually instantiate dependencies.

---

# Class Size Guidelines

Recommended maximum:

- Controller: < 200 lines
- Use Case: < 150 lines
- Repository: < 200 lines

If a class becomes too large:

- Extract responsibilities.
- Create new Use Cases.
- Create helper services.

---

# Code Review Checklist

Before writing code, verify:

- Does the class have only one responsibility?
- Can new behavior be added without modifying existing code?
- Can implementations be replaced safely?
- Is the interface minimal?
- Does the code depend on abstractions instead of implementations?
- Is business logic isolated inside Use Cases?
- Is the controller thin?
- Is the repository only responsible for persistence?

If any answer is "No", refactor the code.